 "dur": 45.0,
 "target": 45.0,
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Why did a bicycle cop chase Walter Arnold for five miles?",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 3.299
  },
  {
   "text": "Arnold's horseless carriage is smoking through Kent at eight miles an hour.",
   "s": 3.599,
   "e": 7.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Four times the limit. If he's caught, he's history's first speeder.",
   "s": 7.4,
   "e": 10.399
  },
  {
   "text": "Because in 1896, cars must stay under two miles an hour.",
   "s": 10.599,
   "e": 13.944
  },
  {
   "text": "Worse, a man must walk ahead of every car, waving a red flag.",
   "s": 14.144,
   "e": 17.268
  },
  {
   "text": "Arnold has no flag man. So the constable jumps on his bike.",
   "s": 17.468,
   "e": 20.798
  },
  {
   "text": "Farmers laugh as a bicycle chases a car.",
   "s": 20.998,
   "e": 23.177
  },
  {
   "text": "Then it gets worse: mud, hills, and one furious goose.",
   "s": 23.377,
   "e": 25.809
  },
  {
   "text": "Until the car stops at a gate, and the bicycle wins.",
   "s": 26.209,
   "e": 28.799
  },
  {
   "text": "In court, the charge sheet reads: driving a locomotive without a horse.",
   "s": 28.999,
   "e": 32.608
  },
  {
   "text": "Why such a silly law? Lawmakers feared cars would terrify every horse.",
   "s": 32.808,
   "e": 36.545
  },
  {
   "text": "Months later, England scraps the flag man, and cars race to Brighton.",
   "s": 36.745,
   "e": 39.941
  },
  {
   "text": "Arnold's speeding fine? One shilling, to a man on a bicycle.",
   "s": 40.141,
   "e": 43.332
  }
 ],
